\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Network Performance Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In this course we will introduce you to the tools for design and analysis
 of telecommunications networks.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The core tools of this analysis are probability based: there is an assumption
 that the traffic in the system is stochastic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The techniques we will cover are applicable beyond the design and analysis
 of telecommunication networks.
 
\emph on
e.g.

\emph default
 compute servers dealing with submitted jobs and databases handling queries,
 cashpoints queues, supermarket queues, telephone call centres 
\emph on
etc
\emph default
..
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Practical Considerations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Broadly speaking we need to focus on two interrelated areas in our analysis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Design Issues
\series default
: when building or buying a router how much memory should we allow for?
 How much bandwidth do we need between routers? How much processing power
 should the router have so that it may exploit that bandwidth effectively?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Quality Issues
\series default
: what will be the average delay for end users given our network design?
 What is the worst case delay? How likely is the system to drop packets?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are important because resources are finite, there are limitations
 on the available bandwidth, processor speeds and memory.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Terminology
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A series of communications between two users/computers is known as a 
\emph on
session
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The interactions themselves consist of 
\emph on
messages
\emph default
 each of which may be split into 
\emph on
packets
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Session Characteristics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Characteristics of the session vary according to protocol:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Message arrival rate
\series default
.
 We can seek to model the arrivals as: 
\emph on
Poisson arrivals
\emph default
, 
\emph on
deterministic arrivals
\emph default
, 
\emph on
uniform arrivals
\emph default
 or as coming from a more general class of distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Session holding time
\series default
: The length of a session.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Expected message length
\series default
 and 
\series bold
length distribution
\series default
, message lengths can be modeled as 
\emph on
exponential distributions
\emph default
,
\emph on
 uniform length
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Allowable delay
\series default
 - acceptable expected delays vary.
 The acceptable delay is low for VoIP and quite high for FTP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Reliability
\series default
 - FTP 
\emph on
must
\emph default
 be error free but VoIP can have some bit errors or packet loss without
 major consequences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Message and packet ordering
\series default
 - Some sessions will require that the packets arrive in the order they
 are sent: for example database requests.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In this course we will be particularly interested in the first five of these
 characteristics.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Network Types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Circuit\InsetSpace ~
Switching: used for telephone routing.
 Guarantees an open `channel' between two peers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Packet\InsetSpace ~
Switching or Store and Forward Switching: used for data.
 May operate via 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
Dynamic\InsetSpace ~
Routing Each packet of the message moves through the network independentl
y.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Virtual\InsetSpace ~
Circuit\InsetSpace ~
Routing A fixed path through the network is provided for the
 entire message.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The character of the two approaches are quite different.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Circuit\InsetSpace ~
Switching
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
wasteful in terms of bandwidth.
 Nodes may not be using all the available bandwidth reserved for them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a guaranteed channel is provided with a known communication delay associated
 with it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the sum of rates for all sessions using a link cannot exceed the total capacity
 of the link.
 New sessions must be rejected when the link capacity is reached.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
used for telephone networks where the session rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{s}$
\end_inset

, is the same for each session.
 Switching would be more complex for data networks and the nature of transmissio
n would also mean that the links are inefficiently used.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Packet Switching
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Packet switching makes efficient use of bandwidth because, whilst communication
 between two nodes may be idle for a period of time, packets from another
 communication may be sent along the same line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Because it is unknown 
\emph on
a priori
\emph default
 how much bandwidth will be available it is difficult to guarantee a maximum
 delay.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Communication Media
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Circuit switching networks may share a communication through multiplexing
 (
\emph on
e.g.

\emph default
 time division multiplexing or frequency division multiplexing.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Both are not 100% efficient (time multiplexing requires a small amount of
 time for switching to occur, and frequency division multiplexing requires
 a small `buffer' band of frequencies between each session.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Packet switching networks dedicate communication bandwidth to each channel
 as required.
 In a simple `first in, first out'
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard
Also known as `first come, first served' (FCFS).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (FIFO) system, packets arrive and, if the queue is empty, they are transmitted.
 Otherwise they are stored and await their turn for transmission.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In packet switching there are two delays: a queueing delay and a transmission
 delay.
 The queueing delay is variable, transmission delay will be fixed .
\end_layout

