\begin_layout Itemize
Interarrival time: time between arrivals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Think of buses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

, are continuous values, what is their PDF?.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Interarrival Distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Example 3, Chapter 4.
 Derivation of Interarrival Time Distribution
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/interArrivals.eps

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Caption
For a Poisson process, this figure shows the times of the arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{n}$
\end_inset

, and the interarrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:poissonInterArrival}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the figure above we show different arrival times 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1},\dots,T_{n}$
\end_inset

 for the process.
 You can think of these as the arrival times of the buses.
 We are interested in computing the distribution of the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=T_{i}-T_{i-1}$
\end_inset

: the times between bus arrivals.
 In this example you will derive this PDF for the interarrival times
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard
The interarrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

, are continuous values, so they will be governed by a probability density
 function rather than a discrete probability distribution.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly we define the number of arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to be given by the function 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Next we note that the cumulative density function (CDF) of the interarrival
 time distribution will be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)$
\end_inset

 (see Section\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset

 for a reminder on CDFs).
 We can re-write this probability as
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)=1-P\left(X_{i}>t\right),\]

\end_inset

so, rather than focussing on 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)$
\end_inset

, we can consider the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}>t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Now we rewrite
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Standard
The `
\begin_inset Formula $\equiv$
\end_inset

' sign should be read as `is equivalent to'.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the condition in this distribution as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\left(X_{i}>t\right) & \equiv & \left(T_{i}-T_{i-1}>t\right)\nonumber \\
 & \equiv & \left(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t\right),\label{eq:conditionInterarrival}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where we have simply used the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 and rearranged the inequality.
 Let's take the time that 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i-1}$
\end_inset

 occurs to be 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i-1}$
\end_inset

.
 This lets us write the condition (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:conditionInterarrival}

\end_inset

) as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 This will be true if and only if the function 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 does not change during the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(t_{i-1},t_{i-1}+t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We can express this mathematically as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)\equiv\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right).\]

\end_inset

The right hand side of this statement expresses the condition that no arrivals
 occur between time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i-1}=T_{i-1}$
\end_inset

 and time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)=P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right)\]

\end_inset

now, because of the similarity of the arrival distributions for intervals
 of identical lengths (Section\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset

) we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)\right)=P\left(N\left(t\right)\right)\]

\end_inset

which means that
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right)=P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)\]

\end_inset

and substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)=k=0$
\end_inset

 into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset

) we find that the probability of their being no arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)$
\end_inset

, is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)=\exp\left(-\lambda t\right)\]

\end_inset

so
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}>t\right)=\exp\left(-\lambda t\right)\]

\end_inset

and the CDF we are interested in is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right) & = & 1-P\left(X_{i}>t\right)\\
 & = & 1-\exp\left(-\lambda t\right).\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now to obtain the PDF we simply differentiate the CDF (Section\InsetSpace ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset

) giving
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(X_{i}\right)=\lambda e^{-\lambda X_{i}}\]

\end_inset

which is the interarrival time distribution.
 The form of the distribution is recognised as an 
\emph on
exponential distribution
\emph default
.
 The parameter of the exponential, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, is the rate parameter from the Poisson process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section we have outlined the proof that the interarrival times for
 a Poisson process are distributed exponentially.
 Hereafter, we will use these formulations interchangeably, 
\emph on
i.e.
 
\emph default
when we state that the interarrival times for students registering at the
 Goodwin Sports centre is given by an exponential distribution with parameter
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset

 we are equivalently stating that these students are arriving according
 to a Poisson process with rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Properties of the Exponential Distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Memoryless property
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X>s+t|X>t\right)=P\left(X>s\right)\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In English, the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset

 given that the waiting time is already greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is the same as the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Mean and Variance
\begin_inset Formula \[
p(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The mean of an exponential can be found to be, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}=\frac{1}{\lambda}\]

\end_inset

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle _{p(x)}-\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}^{2}=\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}.\]

\end_inset

 These are easily confirmed --- they are part of Tutorial 1.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
