Time and Steady State Averages II
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The derivation of Little's formula relies on an assumption.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Assume that averages under the steady state distribution are equivalent
 to the time average in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Average number in the system as a function of time is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{t}=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau\]

\end_inset

and the time average after in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\bar{N}\left(t\right).\]

\end_inset

which we assume is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle k\right\rangle _{p\left(k\right)}\equiv\bar{N}$
\end_inset

.
 See pg 156-157 in 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citep{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
The Formula
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Applies under steady state conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Under the steady state assumptions it states,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Example
\series default
: Nightclub queue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You are driving past a nightclub, wondering how long you will have to wait
 to go in.
 You notice on Thursday nights that the queue typically contains 20 people
 and that people arrive at a rate of 40 per hour.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to Little's formula, how long would you expect to have to wait
 to get in? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Answer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard


\backslash
answer{
\end_layout

\end_inset

The waiting time is given by Little's formula: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=20$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=40$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}=\frac{\bar{N}}{\lambda}=\frac{20}{40}=\frac{1}{2}\textrm{hour}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard

}{6}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Intuition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we see there are twenty people in a queue and they enter a building at
 a rate of 40 per hour, we might expect to have to wait half-an-hour in
 the queue.
 Formally though, we will build upon the intuition with a simple graphical
 `proof' of the formula based upon that given in 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citep{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

