\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Little's Formula
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Applies regardless of the arrival and service time distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Relies on the system being in 
\emph on
steady state
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Can be used at the level of the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Or the level of the queue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Or the level of service.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Average Delay in 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The mean of the steady state distribution for an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue is
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu-\lambda}=\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Can use Little's formula to tell us the average delay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
From 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}$
\end_inset

 we rearrange to recover 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\mu-\lambda}\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This is not the time spent in the queue! 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This is the time spent in the system: it includes
\emph on
 service time.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
arises from 
\emph on
propagation delay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
length of phone call 
\emph on
etc
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Little's Formula for Queueing Time and Transmission Time 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The total delay can be divided into 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
queueing time, 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
and service time 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
T=X+W.\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Similarly the average delay may be decomposed as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The queue and transmission may the be analysed independently using Little's
 formula.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
The Transmission
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Assume that customers/packets do not leave the queue.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Rate of arrivals at the queue must equal the rate of arrivals at transmission.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We define the the average number under transmission as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\lambda\bar{X},\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 is the average time spend under transmission for each packet.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue the average transmission time is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
So for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{\lambda}{\mu},\]

\end_inset

 a formula which we have already used in the last chapter.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Utilisation Factor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is the utilisation factor.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Represents the fraction of time that the transmission line (or server) is
 in use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is a measure of efficiency.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is also the steady state probability that the transmission line is in
 use.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a lossless system this probability is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $1-P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Utilisation Example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Example 2, Chapter 7.
 Simple example of Little's formula.
 You are at a queue in a bar and notice that the bar tender spends 10% of
 her time cleaning between customers.
 If the typical number of people waiting to be served is 3 what is the ratio
 of time spent serving to time being served?
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Answer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard


\backslash
answer{
\end_layout

\end_inset

Information says 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=0.9$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}=3$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda=\frac{0.9}{\bar{X}}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
3=\lambda\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\bar{W}}{\bar{X}}=3\frac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Standard

}{6}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
The Queue
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For arrival rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}$
\end_inset

, the average number in queue, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}$
\end_inset

, the average queueing (waiting) time.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda\bar{W}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\bar{W}=\frac{1}{\mu}+\bar{W}\]

\end_inset

 so the average time spent waiting in the queue is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{T}-\frac{1}{\mu}=\frac{\rho}{\mu-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Average number in the queue is then
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\frac{\rho^{2}}{1-\rho}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Other Markov Queues
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue is perhaps the simplest Markov queue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We will introduce two related Markov queues and compute their stationary
 distributions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A simple modification to the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue: a 
\emph on
finite length 
\emph default
modification 
