 to wait in the queue for service.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset

 system there is no queue because the maximum state of the system is the
 same as the number of servers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The probability of waiting is therefore zero.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The probability of blocking is of interest: the probability that when we
 arrive the system is full and we are denied service.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
As we discussed for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset

 queue the blocking probability is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(m\right)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \[
B(m,a)=P\left(m\right)=\frac{\frac{a^{m}}{m!}}{\sum_{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}.\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use Erlang B curves to compute.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Carried Load and Utilization Factors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since traffic is lost in 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset

 the carried load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, is smaller than the offered load.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The carried load is given by the offered load multiplied by the probability
 that the packet is not blocked.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a^{\prime}=a(1-P\left(m\right))=a(1-B(m,a))\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The carried load per line is the trunk occupancy (the utilization facctor),
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{a^{\prime}}{m}.\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Note the contrast with the lossless systems where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{a}{m}\]

\end_inset

because the carried load is equal to the offered load 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}=a$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This also applies for the other system with loss we have seen, the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset

 queue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The rate of service in the system is no longer given by the arrival rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, we need to consider those packets that are lost.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\dot{\lambda}=\lambda(1-P\left(m\right))\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This has effects when studying the queue using Little's formula.
\end_layout

