\begin_layout Caption
Computation of residual service time for system with vacations.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For this system 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}r\left(\tau\right)d\tau & = & \frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}\frac{1}{2}X_{i}^{2}+\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}\frac{1}{2}V_{i}^{2}\\
 & = & \frac{M\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}X_{i}^{2}}{M\left(t\right)}+\frac{L\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}V_{i}^{2}}{L\left(t\right)}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

arrival process, 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

, vacation process, 
\begin_inset Formula $L\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Average of Vacation Distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This means we have to deal with the average number of vacations.
 On average the proportion of time available for vacations is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-\rho\right)$
\end_inset

, 
\emph on
i.e.
 
\emph default
that time where the transmission line is not in use.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the average length of a vacation at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}_{t}$
\end_inset

 then the number of vacations that will have arrived at that time is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
L\left(t\right)=\frac{t\left(1-\rho\right)}{\bar{V}_{t}}.\]

\end_inset

Recalling 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}r\left(\tau\right)d\tau=\frac{M\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}X_{i}^{2}}{M\left(t\right)}+\frac{L\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}V_{i}^{2}}{L\left(t\right)}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Taking the limit of as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 we can write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{R}=\frac{1}{2}\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle +\frac{\left(1-\rho\right)\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle }{2\bar{V}}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
P-K Formula
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
So given that the assumptions that we used to develop the formula 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset

are still valid, we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle }{2\bar{V}}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset

 with Vacations --- Simple example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A bar has Poisson arrivals and one member of staff serving.
 When there are no customers at the bar, the staff member leaves the bar
 to check on the kitchen.
 Assuming these checks are exponentially distributed in length, with mean
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

 and that customer service times are exponentially distributed with mean
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 what is the average waiting time in the queue?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Exponential standard deviation = 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

, mean
\begin_inset Formula $=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

.
 So if mean is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

, standard deviation is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

 and variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus second moment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle =2\bar{V}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Similarly for second moment of service time 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =2\bar{X}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Defining 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\bar{X}}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\bar{V}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\bar{V}.\]

\end_inset

Deterministic vacations would have lead to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\bar{X}}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\bar{V}}{2}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\bar{V}}{2}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Networks of Queues and Simulation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
So far in the course we have looked at queueing models in isolation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We will now turn briefly to some of the issues that arise when they are
 combined.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We will start by considering a simple combination of two 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queues.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
`
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

' Queues in Tandem
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:tandemQueuesOne}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consider a simple system involving two transmission lines in tandem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/MM1tandem.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Caption
Two queues in tandem
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Packets have a fixed length and their transmission times are proportional
 to their length.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Packet lengths are exponentially distributed and arrivals occur at the first
 queue as a Poisson process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Implications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The first queue may be modelled as 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

, but the second queue cannot be.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A packet which is large will take a long time in the first queue.
 During this time the second queue will have a chance to clear.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The result will be that for the second queue larger packets are likely to
 arrive when the queue is smaller.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This means that a large packet will typically experience less delay at the
 second queue than a small one.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Analogy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One analogy for this situation is a slow moving vehicle on a road.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
From this vehicle's driver's perception there is very little traffic on
 the road because the slow moving vehicle never catches up with any.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A similar thing occurs with 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queues in tandem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A large packet arrives at the second queue to find it has less packets because
 the smaller packets have had time to be processed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Lack of Independence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The first queue destroys the independence of the packet length and the state
 of the second queue.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This problem is difficult to deal with analytically.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
To proceed with our analysis we will need to break this dependency.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This can be achieved by considering service times for each packet to be
 statistically independent at each queue.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Time Reversibility --- Burke's Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the analysis of the Markov chain systems we have seen so far we relied
 on detailed balance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Detailed balance equations are a special case of global balance equations
 which arise when transitions only occur between neighbouring states in
 the Markov chain.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Detailed balance can be applied to find the stationary distribution in any
 birth-death processes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Burke's theorem leads to an additional special property of birth-death processes
 known as 
\emph on
time reversibility
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Time Reversibility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The principle with time reversibility is as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
for a birth-death process at steady state the arrival process is Poisson
 with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 Time reversibility states that the 
\emph on
departure process
\emph default
 will also be Poisson with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A proof is given in Section\InsetSpace ~
3.7 of Bertsekas and Gallager.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Time Revesibility Intuition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consider the following `intuition' for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The service rate for the system is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The service rate is 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 the departure rate because a departure can only occur when the system has
 a state 
\begin_inset Formula $k>0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This condition is satisfied for a fraction of the time which is given by
 the utilization factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The departure rate is therefore given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mu\rho=\mu\frac{\lambda}{\mu}=\lambda\]

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Burke's Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Burke's theorem follows on from time reversibility.
 It states that 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
if an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

, an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset

 or an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset

 queue at its stationary distribution has a Poisson arrival process with
 a rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
then the departure process is also Poisson with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Furthermore the number of the customers in the system at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
independent
\emph default
 of the sequence of departure times prior to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Queues with loss are slightly more complex because the arrival process does
 not have rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 due to blocked packets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Burke's Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The second property is worthy of comment as it seems counterintuitive.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You might expect that if there were a rapid series of departures from the
 queue then the queue would be shorter than normal,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Burke's theorem tells us that this is not the case.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
From this we can infer that the state of a queue 
\emph on
before 
\emph default
a rapid series of departures is about to occur must be abnormally high.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Otherwise it 
\emph on
would
\emph default
 be abnormally low after the departures which Burke's theorem tells us is
 not the case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 Queues in Tandem Revisited
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Lets return to the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queues in tandem.
 --- Example 2 in notes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Burke's theorem tells us that the departure process from the first queue
 will be a Poisson with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We allow service times at the second queue to be independent of service
 times at the first queue.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This breaks the dependence of the queue length at the second queue on the
 service time at the second queue.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In this case the system can be modelled as two 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queues.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
M/M/1 in Tandem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the service time distributions at the two queues are governed by exponential
 distributions with rate parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This leads to utilization factors for each queue as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu_{1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{2}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu_{2}}$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The distributions for the states of the systems are given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k_{1}\right)=\rho_{1}^{k_{1}}\left(1-\rho_{1}\right)\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k_{2}\right)=\rho_{2}^{k_{2}}\left(1-\rho_{2}\right).\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The joint distribution for both systems is then
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
p\left(k_{1},k_{2}\right) & = & p\left(k_{1}\right)p\left(k_{2}\right)\\
 & = & \rho_{1}^{k_{1}}\left(1-\rho_{1}\right)\rho_{2}^{k_{2}}\left(1-\rho_{2}\right)\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

because the two queues can be viewed independently.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
As a result the average delay in the system can be computed as the sum of
 the average delays in each queue
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{T}_{1}+\bar{T}_{2}=\frac{1}{\mu_{1}-\lambda}+\frac{1}{\mu_{2}-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Relation to Reality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This will typically be larger than the actual delay because
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
when the queue length is correlated with packet size 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
a large packet will find the second queue to be smaller than normal 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
and would therefore spend less time than might be expected in the system.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Kleinrock's Independence Approximation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Kleinrock's independence approximation suggests assuming independence for
 every node in the network.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The approximation is good if if large numbers of packets from outside the
 network are introduced at every queue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consider a general network with labelled nodes and links between these nodes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are several packet streams in the network, each stream has a rate
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{s}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We define 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{ij}\left(s\right)$
\end_inset

 as the fraction of packets from stream 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 that cross the link between 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The rate associated with a link between node 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\lambda_{ij} & = & \sum_{s\textrm{ }}f_{ij}\left(s\right)x_{s}.\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
Kleinrock's Independence Approximation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Kleinrock suggested that merging traffic streams on a transmission line
 has the effect of restoring independence of arrival and service times.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The approach is to assume that each link can be treated as an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue.
 This is a good approximation for 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
arrivals which occur as a Poisson process 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
exponentially distributed packets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
densely connected networks 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
moderate-heavy traffic.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Given this assumption, from the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset

 queue, we know that the average number at each link is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{ij}=\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}-\lambda_{ij}}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout ShortFoilhead
The Average in the System
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus the average number in the entire system is
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{N} & = & \sum_{ij}\bar{N}_{ij}\\
 & = & \sum_{ij}\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}-\lambda_{ij}}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Average delay at each link is given by Little's theorem
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}_{ij}=\frac{1}{\mu_{ij}-\lambda_{ij}}.\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

